solution.teaching:
A binary search tree (BST) keeps every key in a node's left subtree smaller than the node and every key in its right subtree larger. Inserting a key means walking down from the root — left if smaller, right if larger — until an empty spot is found; the insertion order therefore fixes the shape of the tree.

The three depth-first traversals differ only in *when* a node is visited relative to its two subtrees:
- **pre-order**: node, left, right;
- **in-order**: left, node, right — for a BST this lists the keys in sorted order;
- **post-order**: left, right, node — every node appears after all of its descendants, so the root comes last.

A useful counting fact: in post-order the entire left subtree of the root is visited first, and it ends with its own root. Here the keys smaller than 40 are $25, 33, 10, 5, 36, 18, 28$ — exactly 7 of them — so the 7th post-order key must be the root of the left subtree, $25$. The full traversal confirms this.

answerVerification.checkCode:
keys = [40, 61, 25, 33, 10, 70, 52, 5, 36, 18, 66, 28]
tree = {}                                              # key -> [left, right]
root = None
for k in keys:
    tree[k] = [None, None]
    if root is None: root = k; continue
    cur = root
    while True:
        side = 0 if k < cur else 1
        if tree[cur][side] is None: tree[cur][side] = k; break
        cur = tree[cur][side]
def post(n):
    return [] if n is None else post(tree[n][0]) + post(tree[n][1]) + [n]
def inorder(n):
    return [] if n is None else inorder(tree[n][0]) + [n] + inorder(tree[n][1])
def pre(n):
    return [] if n is None else [n] + pre(tree[n][0]) + pre(tree[n][1])
p = post(root); print(p, p[6])                         # 7th = 25
assert p[6] == 25 and inorder(root) == sorted(keys)
print(inorder(root)[6], pre(root)[6])                  # distractors: 36 (in-order), 28 (pre-order)
